{
 "deepseek": "The experiment is well-designed and the data strongly support the preregistered hypotheses H-008 and H-009, with some caveats about the sliding window not being active and the need for specificity controls. The implementation appears correct, with only a minor potential prereg deviation regarding the in-sample/held-out split assumption. The statistical analysis follows the prereg, though the bootstrap over pairs may be anti-conservative due to shared items. The conclusions are warranted at the median level for this model and these relations.",
 "minimax": "The supplied implementation and data support a clean preregistered operational result: on 28 held-out pairs, all five threshold criteria P1-P5 and the NULL control pass. The strongest finding is that layer-13 shared K/V, particularly at the differing subject span, is sufficient to restore answer discrimination while layer-14 shared K/V alone is not. Block 25 is likewise strongly implicated because replacing its attention contribution nearly restores the clean output and ATTN_25 dominates the layer sweep. The conclusions should nevertheless remain bounded to the selected tasks and operational interventions: the data strongly support sufficiency and localization, but they do not prove that block 25 is the unique semantic transfer site or that it is logically necessary. The principal implementation defect is that the analysis does not enforce the preregistered NULL-invalid and underpowered status rules, although neither defect affects the supplied run."
}
